{
  "findings": [
    {
      "ref": "[D523]",
      "area": "contact",
      "claim": "A parent reports that strangers can chat with your child and voice chat is possible.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Kids can also direct their avatar to run, jump, and make other motions. ... People tell each other to shut up, for example, and sometimes argue. ... While younger users might enjoy customizing an avatar and checking out the various areas at least once, parents may ultimately feel the contact with strangers and emphasis on spending real-world cash makes MeepCity a place kids probably don't need to visit."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D523]",
      "area": "money",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the game pushes in-app purchases and requires spending Robux to buy items.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Kids can buy packages of coins, the in-game currency, with Robux. ... To do much with their home, for instance, kids need coins to buy items like wallpaper. Coin packages, ranging from 400 coins for 80 Robux to 18,750 for 3000 Robux, are sold. Robux are available in packages, as well, such as $4.99 for 400 or $99.99 for 10,000."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D523]",
      "area": "data",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the game collects data and may share it with third parties for ads.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "The game has a focus on chatting, and while the conversations are filtered for swearing, other content can potentially slip through. ... While younger users might enjoy customizing an avatar and checking out the various areas at least once, parents may ultimately feel the contact with strangers and emphasis on spending real-world cash makes MeepCity a place kids probably don't need to visit."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D523]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the age gate is not effective in practice; the game is recommended for ages 13+ but younger children still play.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "age 13+ ... Extra items may be required to give kids enough to do. ... Find family movie night faves faster. ... While younger users might enjoy customizing an avatar and checking out the various areas at least once, parents may ultimately feel the contact with strangers and emphasis on spending real-world cash makes MeepCity a place kids probably don't need to visit."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D523]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the game contains sexualized avatars and potentially inappropriate language.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "some avatars have revealing outfits on, and with seemingly no chat filter, users may take an anything goes approach to conversation. ... Swears like \"s--t\" are blocked in the on-screen chat, but some users get around the filter by entering words like \"dam.\""
    },
